# Expectation record for 08_gcc_extensions/006_builtin_functions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_builtin_functions
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = Documented builtin functions with deterministic results
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every bit-counting builtin is given a nonzero argument, so the cases those builtins leave
undefined for zero are never reached.  The string and memory builtins operate on
properly terminated literals and on distinct objects large enough for the access, so no
read or write is out of bounds and no overlap is relied upon.  All results are printed as
fixed-width integers, so no printed value depends on a target's word size.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
bi_popcount32=16
bi_popcount64=13
bi_clz_one=31
bi_clz_top=0
bi_ctz_eight=3
bi_clzll_one=63
bi_ctzll_bit40=40
bi_bswap16=13330
